WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.013
Picture opening a small restaurant kitchen every morning by phoning each cook with orders.

2
00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:10.060
Light the oven.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.455
Now start the sauce.

4
00:00:11.455 --> 00:00:12.851
Now open the pantry.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:13.623
It works.

6
00:00:13.623 --> 00:00:17.707
Until the day you are ill, and nobody else knows the order.

7
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:24.140
Or the cook starts the sauce before the oven is hot, because lit is not the same as ready.

8
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:28.585
That is exactly how we started hello-devops and Redis last time.

9
00:00:28.585 --> 00:00:33.170
A network, a volume, and two long commands, typed in the right order.

10
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.764
Phone calls, one by one.

11
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.613
The fix is a plan pinned to the kitchen wall.

12
00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:47.213
In Docker, that plan is one file, compose.yaml, read by Docker Compose, a tool that runs an app made of several containers.

13
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.037
Under services, one entry per station.

14
00:00:50.037 --> 00:00:54.339
Web, built from our Dockerfile, and Redis, a ready-made image.

15
00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:58.919
Under volumes, the pantry: a named volume that keeps Redis's data.

16
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:01.260
You do not even write the network.

17
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:07.710
Compose makes one for the project, puts every service on it, and so service names work as addresses.

18
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.775
Web finds Redis simply as redis.

19
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:13.184
Only web publishes a port, 8000, to your Mac.

20
00:01:13.184 --> 00:01:17.157
Redis has no port at all, because only web talks to it.

21
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:18.439
Notice the switch.

22
00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:20.773
No more phone calls telling each step.

23
00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:25.503
You describe the finished kitchen, and docker compose up works out the steps.

24
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.037
Now the trap from the opening.

25
00:01:28.037 --> 00:01:32.109
We want Redis started before web, so we add depends on, redis.

26
00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.343
Surely that means: wait for Redis.

27
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:36.328
Let's be fair and watch it.

28
00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:37.725
Compose starts Redis.

29
00:01:37.725 --> 00:01:39.387
Its container is running.

30
00:01:39.387 --> 00:01:44.373
But a database can need several more seconds before it accepts connections.

31
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:47.027
The short form only waits for running.

32
00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:51.096
So web starts right away, calls Redis, and nobody answers yet.

33
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:54.217
The sauce went in, and the oven was only lit.

34
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:56.750
The fix is a healthcheck.

35
00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:04.963
Docker runs redis-cli ping inside the container every five seconds, with a three second timeout and five retries.

36
00:02:04.963 --> 00:02:07.071
Exit code zero means healthy.

37
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:10.142
And the long form of depends on waits for it.

38
00:02:10.142 --> 00:02:11.867
Condition: service healthy.

39
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:13.823
Run docker compose up.

40
00:02:13.823 --> 00:02:16.616
The volume and network are created.

41
00:02:16.616 --> 00:02:20.766
Redis warms up, and turns healthy after 5.8 seconds.

42
00:02:20.766 --> 00:02:23.400
Only then does web start, at 6.0.

43
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.897
Call the app three times, and the visit count goes one, two, three, all kept by Redis.

44
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:30.503
So here is the rule.

45
00:02:30.503 --> 00:02:32.010
Running is not ready.

46
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.164
Make the app wait for healthy.

47
00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:35.715
Closing time.

48
00:02:35.715 --> 00:02:37.225
docker compose down.

49
00:02:37.225 --> 00:02:40.699
What happens to the visit count, three so far?

50
00:02:40.699 --> 00:02:41.907
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:47.056
Down removes the containers and the network.

52
00:02:47.056 --> 00:02:48.755
The counters get cleared.

53
00:02:48.755 --> 00:02:50.589
But the named volume stays.

54
00:02:50.589 --> 00:02:51.880
That is the pantry.

55
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:55.626
Bring it back up, and the count carries on where it left off.

56
00:02:55.626 --> 00:02:57.435
It does not start again at one.

57
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:04.519
But add two characters, dash v, and Compose deletes the named volumes too, and your data with them, for good.

58
00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:07.202
Two commands that look almost the same.

59
00:03:07.202 --> 00:03:09.217
Plain down clears the counters.

60
00:03:09.217 --> 00:03:11.492
Down dash v empties the pantry too.

61
00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:13.520
Two honest limits.

62
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.888
A healthcheck only covers startup.

63
00:03:15.888 --> 00:03:22.574
If Redis dies later, hello-devops answers its readiness check with a 503, and the app must cope.

64
00:03:22.574 --> 00:03:25.568
And Compose runs everything on one machine.

65
00:03:25.568 --> 00:03:28.215
If that machine dies, so does the app.

66
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:30.601
So the moral of the story.

67
00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:35.540
One file is the plan on the wall, and one command makes the kitchen match it.

68
00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.721
Wait for healthy, not for running.

69
00:03:37.721 --> 00:03:40.864
And down keeps the pantry, unless you say dash v.

70
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.232
Quick check.

71
00:03:42.232 --> 00:03:47.362
A compose file says depends on redis, the short form, with no healthcheck.

72
00:03:47.362 --> 00:03:48.609
What can go wrong?

73
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.930
Web can start before Redis accepts connections.

74
00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.061
The sauce goes into a cold oven.
